Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Material Adverse Effect' means any effect, change, event, circumstance, condition, occurrence or development that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, properties, assets, liabilities, results of operations or financial condition of such party and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of such party to timely consummate the transactions contemplated hereby; provided, however, that, with respect to clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to be or constitute a 'Material Adverse Effect': (a) changes in general economic conditions or conditions in the financial markets, credit markets or capital markets; (b) changes in regulatory, legislative or political conditions; (c) any outbreak or escalation of hostilities or acts of war; (d) pandemics or other public health events; (e) any change in accounting standards or applicable law; provided further that, with respect to clause (ii), such effects shall only be considered a Material Adverse Effect if they disproportionately adversely affect such party as compared to other similar businesses in the same industry. (Pages 45-46)\nAnswer:
Choices:

