Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Company Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, circumstance, or effect (each, an “Effect”) that, individually or together with other Effects, (a) has a material adverse effect on the financial condition, business, assets, or operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (b) would prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided that, for clause (a) only, none of the following will be considered a Company Material Adverse Effect: (i) changes in economic conditions affecting the global market; (ii) changes in the regulatory environment affecting the industry, provided such changes do not disproportionately affect the Company; (iii) acts of God, including natural disasters, which do not have a sustained impact beyond three months; (iv) any changes in the stock market in general; (v) any geopolitical tensions that do not directly involve the Company; (vi) any failure by the Company to meet financial projections, unless such failure is due to internal mismanagement; and (vii) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement or the transactions contemplated hereby. Furthermore, for clause (b), the following will not be considered in determining a Company Material Adverse Effect: (viii) any effect resulting from compliance with this Agreement; (ix) any adverse effect arising from the identity of the Parent as the acquirer; (x) any action taken by the Company with the prior written consent of the Parent. (Pages 12-13)\nAnswer:
Choices:

